require ["fileinto", "mailbox"];

# Vectis global spam-filing rule (sieve_before).
#
# Files spam into Junk before any per-user sieve, so it never reaches INBOX,
# then stops. The `stop` ends Sieve processing for the message, so a per-user
# script never sees flagged mail and CANNOT move it back out of Junk. To keep a
# wanted sender in INBOX, add the sender or domain to the domain's spam allow
# list (Domains -> Spam lists in the admin UI). That is a strong hint, not an
# override: for a sender that passes SPF or DMARC it applies a large negative
# score, which normally keeps the message under the spam threshold so our
# X-Spam header is never set. A very high-scoring message can still be flagged,
# and an upstream X-Spam-Flag (below) is still honoured.
#
# Matches two independent spam flags:
#   - X-Spam: Yes       — set by our rspamd (milter_headers "spam-header") on
#                         messages at/above the add_header (spam) threshold.
#   - X-Spam-Flag: YES  — the SpamAssassin-standard flag. Our rspamd does NOT
#                         emit this, so it only appears on mail already tagged
#                         by an UPSTREAM scanner — e.g. a cPanel host forwarding
#                         mail in. Forwarded mail is re-scored low by rspamd
#                         (trusted relay) and so never gets our X-Spam header;
#                         honouring the upstream flag keeps that pre-tagged spam
#                         out of INBOX. (Worst case if forged: a sender files
#                         their own mail to Junk — harmless.)
#
# Wired in via `sieve_before` in dovecot.conf only when rspamd.file_spam_to_junk
# is enabled (the default). Mounted read-only; Pigeonhole compiles the .svbin
# in the container's own writable layer.
if anyof (
    header :is "X-Spam"      "Yes",
    header :is "X-Spam-Flag" "YES"
) {
    fileinto :create "Junk";
    stop;
}
